        "questionText": "‘Johannes Gutenberg’s printing press was the main reason why medical knowledge advanced in the years c1500–c1700.’ How far do you agree? Explain your answer.",
        "stimulus": [
          "Printed anatomical textbooks",
          "The Royal Society (1660)"
        ],
        "sourceExtract": "",
        "modelAnswer": "<p>The invention and spread of the printing press from the mid-fifteenth century broke the Catholic Church’s monopoly over copying medical manuscripts and enabled rapid, accurate communication across Europe. Scholars like Andreas Vesalius (1543) and William Harvey (1628) published detailed woodcut anatomical plates and experimental proofs in thousands of identical copies, allowing physicians in England to test and debate challenges to Galen directly.</p><p>However, institutional patronage and scientific societies—especially the Royal Society founded in London in 1660 under King Charles II’s charter—were equally vital in legitimising experimental science (\"Nullius in verba\" — take nobody’s word for it). Publishing its journal Philosophical Transactions from 1665 and demonstrating Robert Hooke’s Micrographia microscope drawings, the Royal Society gave pioneers a respected forum to share empirical discoveries.</p><p>Moreover, Renaissance humanism and individual genius supplied the intellectual courage required to question ancient texts in the first place. Without declining Church censorship after the Reformation, realist Renaissance artistry, and Vesalius’s hands-on human dissections at Padua, printing presses would merely have mass-produced Galen’s errors faster rather than overturning them.</p><p>Overall, while the printing press provided the indispensable communication engine that prevented new discoveries from dying in isolation, I only partially agree: genuine medical advance between c1500 and c1700 depended just as heavily on Renaissance empirical inquiry and institutional backing like the Royal Society.</p>",
