        "modelAnswer": "<p>Dubbed the \"English Hippocrates\", Thomas Sydenham pioneered a major shift away from book-bound Galenic theory toward direct clinical observation at the patient’s bedside. In Observationes Medicae (1676), Sydenham insisted that diseases existed as distinct external species—successfully differentiating scarlet fever from measles—and popularised specific remedies such as cinchona bark (quinine) for malaria and cool air rather than sweating regimens for smallpox.</p><p>However, in everyday practice for most ordinary people in seventeenth-century England, diagnosis and treatment changed remarkably little during Sydenham’s lifetime. Physicians still inspected urine charts, cast astrological horoscopes, and prescribed heroic bloodletting, emetics, and herbal remedies—famously subjecting King Charles II to bleeding, blistering, and crushed human skull powder on his deathbed in 1685.</p><p>Furthermore, Sydenham himself remained trapped by the absence of microbiology, attributing epidemic fevers to seasonal atmospheric miasma rather than germs. Wealthy families continued relying on domestic recipe books compiled by noblewomen like Lady Grace Mildmay alongside apothecaries and quack patent medicines imported from the New World.</p><p>Overall, I disagree that Sydenham transformed seventeenth-century treatment immediately: while his clinical classification method laid the vital long-term foundation for modern medical diagnosis after his death, practical treatments before 1700 remained overwhelmingly traditional and humoral.</p>",
