        "modelAnswer": "<p>Florence Nightingale transformed both physical hospital design and the professional status of nursing across Victorian Britain after slashing mortality at Scutari military hospital in the Crimean War (1854–56). Through Notes on Nursing (1859), Notes on Hospitals (1863), and her £44,000 Nightingale Fund training school at St Thomas’ Hospital (1860), she replaced untrained, drunken attendants with disciplined, salaried nurses and pioneered airy, washable \"pavilion-plan\" wards.</p><p>However, Nightingale still believed in miasma rather than germs, meaning scientific breakthroughs by Pasteur, Lister, and late-nineteenth-century surgeons were vital to stop post-operative ward deaths. Joseph Lister’s carbolic acid antiseptic spray (1865) cut surgical ward mortality from 46% to 15%, evolving by the 1890s into full aseptic operating theatres using steam-sterilised instruments, rubber gloves, and surgical masks.</p><p>Furthermore, government funding and social reform expanded hospital provision far beyond voluntary charities. Following the 1867 Metropolitan Poor Act and 1875 public health reforms, local authorities built tax-funded infirmaries and isolation hospitals for the working poor rather than leaving sick paupers inside grim workhouses.</p><p>Overall, I agree to a large extent: while Lister’s antiseptic and aseptic science conquered surgical infection by 1900, Florence Nightingale was the decisive catalyst who professionalised daily ward nursing, sanitation, and hospital architecture nationwide.</p>",
