        "sourceExtract": "",
        "modelAnswer": "<p>The Act of Uniformity (May 1559) was vital because it shaped Sunday worship in every English parish and prevented religious civil war. By fusing the traditional 1549 Catholic-leaning communion wording with the 1552 Protestant wording inside the 1559 Book of Common Prayer, retaining clerical ornaments, and setting a modest 12-pence (one shilling) recusancy fine, Uniformity enabled moderate Catholics to conform outwardly alongside Protestants.</p><p>Nevertheless, the Act of Supremacy (May 1559) provided the essential constitutional foundation without which liturgical reform could never have stood. Supremacy legally abolished papal authority in England, replaced twenty-six opposing Marian bishops in the House of Lords, and appointed Elizabeth \"Supreme Governor\"—a shrewd title calming Catholic and female-rule critics—backed by a mandatory Oath of Supremacy for all clergy and royal officials.</p><p>Moreover, neither statute would have functioned on the ground without the 57 Royal Injunctions and the summer 1559 Church visitations. Royal commissioners physically removed unlawful stone altars, checked parish licences to preach, and ensured over 95% of parish priests used the new Prayer Book, turning parliamentary parchment into daily local obedience.</p><p>Overall, while the Act of Uniformity brilliantly secured peaceful parish conformity through compromise wording, I disagree that it was the single most important part: the Act of Supremacy was fundamentally more decisive because seizing royal control of Church governance and the bench of bishops made every other part of the 1559 Settlement legally enforceable.</p>",
        "pdfUrl": "",
