        "modelAnswer": "<p>Pope Pius V’s February 1570 Papal Bull, Regnans in Excelsis, fundamentally transformed the ideological threat to Elizabeth by excommunicating her and absolving English Catholics from their oath of allegiance. Whereas conservative Catholics had hesitated during the November 1569 Revolt of the Northern Earls, after 1570 rebelling or assassinating the Queen carried papal blessing—inspiring Douai seminary priests (from 1568), Jesuit missionaries after 1580, and conspirators like Ridolfi (1571), Throckmorton (1583), and Babington (1586).</p><p>However, domestic plots only posed a genuine military threat when backed by Catholic superpowers Spain and France. Because the vast majority of English Catholics remained loyal despite £20 monthly recusancy fines (1581), every post-1570 plot relied entirely on foreign armies: Roberto Ridolfi sought 10,000 Spanish troops from the Duke of Alba, Francis Throckmorton planned a French Guise landing financed by Spain, and Babington depended on a 60,000-strong invasion force.</p><p>Furthermore, the continued presence of Mary, Queen of Scots in English captivity provided the indispensable dynastic figurehead uniting domestic plotters and foreign monarchs, while Sir Francis Walsingham’s spy network repeatedly neutralised those plots before a single foreign soldier landed. Without Mary’s rival Tudor claim, neither the Papal Bull nor Spanish gold had a viable Catholic monarch to place on the throne—proven once Walsingham’s 1586 beer-barrel sting led to Mary’s execution in February 1587.</p><p>Overall, while the 1570 Papal Bull supplied the crucial religious justification that radicalised a minority of Catholic plotters, I disagree that it was the main reason they threatened Elizabeth: foreign military backing from Spain and France—anchored around captive Mary Stuart—was what turned small conspiracies into existential invasion threats.</p>",
